; Html - a templating DSL for HTML, written in the macro system.
;
; A page is built into a builder `b` and finished once:
;
;     (let ((b hNew))
;       {
;         (raw b "<!DOCTYPE html>\n")
;         (html b
;           { (head b { (title b { (text b heading) }) })
;             (body b
;               { (divA b { (class b "card") }
;                   { (h1 b { (text b heading) })
;                     (ul b (for it items (li b { (text b it) }))) }) }) })
;         (hFinish b)
;       })
;
; Every element form is a MACRO, every attribute form is a macro, the
; iteration form is a macro, and the escaping they all reach is a set
; of ordinary functions. Everything escapes by default: `text` and
; every attribute value pass through `hEscText`/`hEscAttr`, `raw` is
; the ONLY way to emit unescaped bytes, and `style`/`script` are
; verbatim except that `</` becomes `<\/`, so a closing tag cannot be
; smuggled out of them.
;
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE PROOF: WHICH CAPABILITY OF docs/macro-system.md EACH PART RESTS
; ON, AND WHICH IT WORKS AROUND
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
;
; 1. AN ELEMENT IS A MACRO OVER AN EXPLICIT BUILDER, AND ITS CHILDREN
;    ARE A `{ ... }` BLOCK. The macro exists rather than a function
;    because it must write the open tag BEFORE its children run, and
;    a function cannot: a function's arguments are evaluated first.
;    That is MAC-EXP-7 - an argument is substituted as SYNTAX and
;    evaluated where its parameter stands, not at all if the template
;    drops it (tests/selfhost/360-macro.ax's double-evaluation term
;    measures it). Delete the macro and write `el` as a function, and
;    the markup comes out inside out. The block is an ordinary
;    expression and a valid argument (self_host/parser.ax: `parseExpr`
;    dispatches TK_LBRACE to `parseBlock`, and MAC-CAP-1's fixture
;    tests/selfhost/362-macro-coverage.ax holds `let mut`, `while` and
;    `set` inside a template's block), which is how a macro of fixed
;    arity takes any number of children.
;
; 2. ITERATION IS A MACRO OVER A BINDER PARAMETER. `(for it items
;    body)` binds `it` for the body, and `it` keeps the CALLER's
;    spelling because a macro parameter in binder position takes the
;    argument's name and is not renamed - MAC-HYG-10, `expBinderParam`
;    in self_host/expand.ax, the rule `Err.ax`'s `try!` depends on and
;    tests/stdlib/371-err-module.ax cannot compile without. The
;    template's own `i` and `n` ARE renamed (MAC-HYG-1/2/3,
;    tests/selfhost/361-macro-hygiene.ax), so a body that names `i`
;    means its own. An argument that is not a bare name is AX3035 at
;    the invocation (tests/diagnostics/590-macro-binder-target.ax).
;
; 3. A FREE IDENTIFIER IN A TEMPLATE MEANS WHAT IT MEANS HERE.
;    `hOpenSolo`, `hClose`, `hAttr`, `hVecLen` and the rest resolve at
;    the definition site - MAC-HYG-6, tests/selfhost/364-macro-
;    definition-site.ax (157) - because import resolution has already
;    spelled them `Html$name` and the expander rewrites the reference
;    to that spelling when this module declares it (MAC-HYG-7). The
;    rule this file keeps because of it: EVERY helper a template names
;    is declared in Html.ax, AND IS `pub`. The `for` templates call
;    `hVecLen` and `hVecStr`, declared below, rather than `Vec`'s own
;    `vecLen` and `vecGetStr`; a name this module merely imports is
;    resolved by the fallback of MAC-HYG-8.4, which declines when two
;    modules declare it, and MAC-CAP-10.5 records what a bare name in
;    a template costs (an entry file's own `strConcat` captured the
;    format lowering's, silently and wrongly). `pub` is measured, not
;    chosen: MAC-LANG-10 judges a template's PRIVATE MACRO at the
;    definition site, but a private FUNCTION named by a template is
;    refused at the invocation - `AX3023 private-name Html::hVecLen`
;    from tests/stdlib/420-html-render.ax while it was private.
;
; 4. ESCAPING IS A RUN-TIME CALL. A macro cannot escape, concatenate
;    or read a literal's bytes at expansion time: MAC-LANG-13 refuses
;    evaluation of user code and the query vocabulary is closed
;    (MAC-CAP-6), so `(text b s)` expands to `(hText b s)` and the
;    escape runs when the page renders. The same rule makes the `</`
;    rewrite in `style`/`script` a run-time guarantee, stated below
;    where a reader might have assumed a compile-time one.
;
; 5. THE TAG TABLE IS WRITTEN OUT BY HAND, two lines per tag. A macro
;    cannot generate a macro - a nested `macro` template is AX3021 at
;    the macro's own line (MAC-CAP-8, tests/diagnostics/510-decl-macro-
;    template-kind.ax) - and there is no variadic EXPRESSION macro: the
;    ellipsis belongs to the rule form, which generates declarations
;    (MAC-LANG-14's decision of 2026-08-15; tests/selfhost/393-macro-
;    ellipsis.ax is all declaration templates). So `(div b k)` and
;    `(divA b attrs k)` are two one-line macros each forwarding to `el`
;    / `elA`, and a tag not in the table is `(el b "tag" k)`.
;
; 6. TWO SPELLINGS PER TAG, because "no attributes" cannot be written
;    `{}` - the empty block is refused (scripts/check-degenerate.sh,
;    `empty-brace`), so `(divA b {} k)` is not writable and `div`
;    exists beside `divA`.
;
; 7. ATTRIBUTES ARE MACROS INSIDE A BLOCK, `{ (class b "x") (id b
;    "y") }`, because `:class "x"` cannot lex: `:` is TK_COLON to
;    self_host/lexer.ax and the parser accepts it in three declaration
;    positions only. One macro per common attribute name, because a
;    macro cannot dispatch on a head's SPELLING (MAC-LANG-17 is P, on
;    scope sets; tests/selfhost/392-macro-patterns.ax's `defOp` is the
;    measurement), and `(attr b "name" v)` for every other name.
;
; 8. TAG AND ATTRIBUTE NAMES ARE STRING LITERALS IN THE TEMPLATES,
;    written a second time beside the macro's own name, because the
;    only source-to-string bridge is `syntax/name` and it takes a bare
;    identifier in expression position (MAC-CAP-5's row) - there is no
;    `syntax/concat` to build `"<div>"` from `div`. Each literal keeps
;    THIS file's byte offsets when a diagnostic lands on it
;    (MAC-EXP-14a, held-but-defective): a type error inside a template
;    may report a span in the user's file computed from Html.ax's
;    bytes. Measured while building this module - AX3018 reported
;    `main.ax:41:19894-19895` in a 50-line file.
;
; NAMES THAT CANNOT BE MACROS, measured rather than listed from taste:
;
;   - the heads self_host/parser.ax dispatches on before the macro
;     table is read: `fn`, `lambda`, `let`, `if`, `match`, `cond`,
;     `while`, `set`, `struct`, `alloc`, `handle`, `cast`, `pub`,
;     `data`, `import`, `macro`, `effect`, `type`, `trait`, `impl`,
;     `extern`, and the reserved `begin`/`consume`/`union`/`region`/
;     `foreign`. So the `<input type=...>` attribute is `(attr b
;     "type" "text")`.
;   - a name any signature in scope uses as a TYPE VARIABLE. A bare
;     identifier naming a macro is a zero-argument invocation
;     (MAC-LANG-3), and the expander reads type positions: a macro
;     named `a` made every `(-> Int a Int)` in Vec.ax report
;     `AX3018 macro a takes 2 arguments, but was given 0`. The
;     standard library's signatures spell `a`, `b`, `e` and `f`, so
;     the anchor element is `anchor`/`anchorA`, never `a`.
;   - `main`: the entry file's `main` would win a bare invocation
;     (MAC-HYG-8.3) and the element would be unreachable by its own
;     name. `<main>` is `(el b "main" k)`.
;   - `title` and `style` are TAGS here, so the attributes of those
;     names are `(attr b "title" v)` and `(attr b "style" v)`; `for`
;     is the iteration form, so `<label for=...>` is `(attr b "for"
;     v)`.
;   - and the rule cuts the other way for a macro of YOUR OWN: a
;     template parameter named like a tag hides that tag inside the
;     template. `(macro (page b body) ... (body b {...}) ...)` does
;     not invoke `<body>` - substitution puts the caller's block where
;     the head stands and the checker reports `expected function type,
;     found Int` at the caller's first child. Measured on
;     examples/web/server.ax's page frame, whose parameter is `kids`.
;
; ONE EXPANSION, END TO END, the way docs/macro-system.md shows them.
; Source:
;
;     (divA b { (class b "card") } { (h1 b { (text b heading) }) })
;
; MAC-EXP-6 step 1 expands each argument. `b` is `b`. The attribute
; block's `(class b "card")` is a macro and becomes
; `(hAttr b "class" "card")`. The child block's `(h1 b {...})` is the
; template `(el b "h1" k)` with `k` the block, re-expanded through
; `el`, so the argument is
;
;     { { (let ((bb b) (tt "h1")) { (hOpenSolo bb tt) { (hText b heading) } (hClose bb tt) }) } }
;
; Step 2 substitutes into `divA`'s template `(elA b "div" attrs k)`,
; and step 3 expands THAT, which is the fixpoint:
;
;     (let ((bb.7 b) (tt.8 "div"))
;       { (hOpen bb.7 tt.8)
;         { (hAttr b "class" "card") }
;         (hOpenEnd bb.7)
;         { { (let ((bb.5 b) (tt.6 "h1"))
;               { (hOpenSolo bb.5 tt.6) { (hText b heading) } (hClose bb.5 tt.6) }) } }
;         (hClose bb.7 tt.8) })
;
; `bb` and `tt` are the template's binders and are gensymed
; (MAC-HYG-3: `.` cannot lex inside an identifier, so no source can
; collide with `bb.7`); `b` and `heading` are the caller's names,
; arriving through parameters and never renamed. The `let` is
; MAC-SAFE-1: the builder and the tag are each mentioned twice in the
; template, and binding them first is what makes `(div (mkPage) ...)`
; build one page rather than two. At run time the calls fire left to
; right and the bytes come out `<div class="card"><h1>...</h1></div>`,
; which the function-shaped `elA` could not produce: its `attrs` and
; `k` would both run before `hOpen`.
;
; MEASURED, so the shape is a decision and not a habit. The builder
; is `Json.ax`'s piece vector plus one measure-then-fill copy in
; `hFinish` (Json.ax `jsonJoin`), never `strConcat` accumulation -
; the quadratic shape tests/net/echo-server.ax builds ON PURPOSE to
; have garbage to reclaim. Bytes the allocator's bump moved to render
; one `<ul>` of N escaped items, read off `__axiom_arena_mark`'s cell
; in a fresh chunk (2026-08-29):
;
;     N     page bytes   pieces + hFinish   strConcat chain
;     20         543          6,736            21,600
;     100      2,623         28,784           419,360
;     1000    26,923        254,224         > 1 MiB (crossed the chunk)
;
; Linear against quadratic: 3.2x at 20 items, 14.6x at 100. The
; per-request arena scope (docs/memory-model.md MM-ALLOC-22) reclaims
; either, which is exactly why the memory gate cannot see the
; difference and this table has to.
;
; WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT PROMISE. `style` and `script` neutralise
; `</` at RUN TIME; nothing here refuses a dangerous literal at
; `check`. `raw` escapes nothing, ever. A builder is single-use:
; `hFinish` answers the document once, and the pieces it copied from
; are garbage for the enclosing arena scope to take. `Pre.ax`'s
; `when`/`unless` are the conditional forms - they answer 0 on the
; untaken arm, which is what a builder call answers too, so
; `(when cond (p b { ... }))` sits in a child block unchanged.
(import Mem)

(import Str)

(import Vec)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The builder
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; A document under construction: a `Vec` of pieces and the running
; byte total, so that `hFinish` allocates the whole document once.
; Built by `hNew`, written through `hPut` and everything above it,
; finished once by `hFinish`.
(pub struct HtmlBuf
  (pieces : (Vec String))
  (mut len : Int))

; A fresh builder. The piece vector is a leaf vector, as `Json.ax`'s
; serialiser keeps its pieces: the pieces are string literals and
; escaped copies, the document copies out of them once, and the arena
; scope around a request is what reclaims them (MM-ALLOC-22).
(pub :: hNew HtmlBuf)

(pub fn (hNew) (HtmlBuf (vecWithCapacity 64) 0))

; Append one piece, verbatim. Every writer in this module ends here;
; nothing is copied until `hFinish`. Answers 0, as every builder call
; does, so a `{ ... }` of them is an `Int` whatever it ends on.
(pub :: hPut (-> HtmlBuf String Int))

(pub fn (hPut b s)
  {
    (vecPush b.pieces s)
    (set b.len (+ b.len (strLen s)))
    0
  }
)

; The bytes written so far.
(pub :: hLen (-> HtmlBuf Int))

(pub fn (hLen b) b.len)

; The document: one allocation of `hLen` bytes, one `memCopy` per
; piece. The builder is spent afterwards - its pieces are garbage, and
; a second call copies them again into a second document.
(pub :: hFinish (-> HtmlBuf String))

(pub fn (hFinish b)
  (let (
    (out (strAlloc b.len))
    (mut k 0)
    (mut off 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< k (vecLen b.pieces))
        (let ((p (vecGetStr b.pieces k)))
          {
            (memCopy (+ (strData out) off) (strData p) (strLen p))
            (set off (+ off (strLen p)))
            (set k (+ k 1))
          }
        ))
      out
    }
  )
)

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Escaping - on by default, five bytes, two contexts.
;
; Two walks over the bytes, exactly `Json.ax`'s `jsonEscapeLen` /
; `jsonEscapeFill`: measure the escaped length, answer the input
; itself when nothing in it needs escaping, otherwise allocate once
; and fill. `&` is in the table first and always, or the `&` of a
; replacement would be a byte to escape again.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The entity for byte `c`, or "" when `c` passes through. Text escapes
; `&`, `<` and `>`; an attribute value escapes `"` and `'` as well,
; because attribute values are always written in double quotes and
; the single quote is belt-and-braces for a template that ever writes
; them the other way.
(:: hEntity (-> Int Bool String))

(fn (hEntity c inAttr)
  (if (== c 38)
    "&amp;"
    (if (== c 60)
      "&lt;"
      (if (== c 62)
        "&gt;"
        (if inAttr
          (if (== c 34)
            "&quot;"
            (if (== c 39)
              "&#39;"
              ""
            )
          )
          ""
        )
      )
    )
  )
)

; `s` with every byte `hEntity` names replaced, or `s` itself when
; there is nothing to replace - the common case for a tag name, a
; class, or a heading, and it costs no allocation.
(:: hEscape (-> String Bool String))

(fn (hEscape s inAttr)
  (let (
    (len (strLen s))
    (mut k 0)
    (mut n 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< k len)
        (let ((e (hEntity (strByte s k) inAttr)))
          {
            (set n (+
              n              (if (== (strLen e) 0)
                1
                (strLen e)
              )
            ))
            (set k (+ k 1))
          }
        ))
      (if (== n len)
        s
        (let (
          (out (strAlloc n))
          (d (strData out))
          (mut i 0)
          (mut off 0)
        )
          {
            (while (< i len)
              (let (
                (c (strByte s i))
                (e (hEntity c inAttr))
              )
                {
                  (if (== (strLen e) 0)
                    {
                      (__store8 d off c)
                      (set off (+ off 1))
                    }
                    {
                      (memCopy (+ d off) (strData e) (strLen e))
                      (set off (+ off (strLen e)))
                    }
                  )
                  (set i (+ i 1))
                }
              ))
            out
          }
        )
      )
    }
  )
)

; Text-context escaping: `&` `<` `>` become `&amp;` `&lt;` `&gt;`.
; Quotes pass through, because text is not inside quotes.
(pub :: hEscText (-> String String))

(pub fn (hEscText s) (hEscape s false))

; Attribute-value escaping: the three above plus `"` as `&quot;` and
; `'` as `&#39;`. What `hAttr` applies to every value it writes.
(pub :: hEscAttr (-> String String))

(pub fn (hEscAttr s) (hEscape s true))

; `s` with every `</` rewritten `<\/`, or `s` itself when it holds
; none. The transform inline CSS and JS go through: a `</style` or
; `</script` inside either would end the element early and hand the
; rest of the string to the HTML parser as markup, and `<\/` means the
; same thing inside a JS string, a regex, a comment and a CSS string.
; A run-time rewrite, not a compile-time refusal (see the header).
(pub :: hEscTagEnd (-> String String))

(pub fn (hEscTagEnd s)
  (let (
    (len (strLen s))
    (mut k 0)
    (mut hits 0)
  )
    {
      (while (< k len)
        {
          (if (&& (== (strByte s k) 60) (== (strByte s (+ k 1)) 47))
            (set hits (+ hits 1))
            0
          )
          (set k (+ k 1))
        })
      (if (== hits 0)
        s
        (let (
          (out (strAlloc (+ len hits)))
          (d (strData out))
          (mut i 0)
          (mut off 0)
        )
          {
            (while (< i len)
              (let ((c (strByte s i)))
                (if (&& (== c 60) (== (strByte s (+ i 1)) 47))
                  {
                    (__store8 d off 60)
                    (__store8 d (+ off 1) 92)
                    (__store8 d (+ off 2) 47)
                    (set off (+ off 3))
                    (set i (+ i 2))
                  }
                  {
                    (__store8 d off c)
                    (set off (+ off 1))
                    (set i (+ i 1))
                  }
                )
              ))
            out
          }
        )
      )
    }
  )
)

; Escaped text: what `(text b s)` expands to.
(pub :: hText (-> HtmlBuf String Int))

(pub fn (hText b s) (hPut b (hEscText s)))

; UNESCAPED bytes: what `(raw b s)` expands to. The only writer here
; that escapes nothing.
(pub :: hRaw (-> HtmlBuf String Int))

(pub fn (hRaw b s) (hPut b s))

; CSS, verbatim except `</` -> `<\/`. What `(style b css)` wraps.
(pub :: hCss (-> HtmlBuf String Int))

(pub fn (hCss b s) (hPut b (hEscTagEnd s)))

; JS, verbatim except `</` -> `<\/`. What `(script b js)` wraps.
(pub :: hJs (-> HtmlBuf String Int))

(pub fn (hJs b s) (hPut b (hEscTagEnd s)))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; Element primitives - the functions the macros call.
;
; The tag name is the author's own literal and is written as it is;
; an attribute NAME likewise. An attribute VALUE is data and goes
; through `hEscAttr`.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; `<tag`, left open for attributes.
(pub :: hOpen (-> HtmlBuf String Int))

(pub fn (hOpen b tag)
  {
    (hPut b "<")
    (hPut b tag)
  }
)

; The `>` that ends an open tag.
(pub :: hOpenEnd (-> HtmlBuf Int))

(pub fn (hOpenEnd b) (hPut b ">"))

; `<tag>` in one step, for an element with no attributes.
(pub :: hOpenSolo (-> HtmlBuf String Int))

(pub fn (hOpenSolo b tag)
  {
    (hPut b "<")
    (hPut b tag)
    (hPut b ">")
  }
)

; `</tag>`.
(pub :: hClose (-> HtmlBuf String Int))

(pub fn (hClose b tag)
  {
    (hPut b "</")
    (hPut b tag)
    (hPut b ">")
  }
)

; ` name="value"`, the value escaped for the attribute context.
(pub :: hAttr (-> HtmlBuf String String Int))

(pub fn (hAttr b name value)
  {
    (hPut b " ")
    (hPut b name)
    (hPut b "=\"")
    (hPut b (hEscAttr value))
    (hPut b "\"")
  }
)

; ` name` alone - a boolean attribute such as `disabled`.
(pub :: hFlag (-> HtmlBuf String Int))

(pub fn (hFlag b name)
  {
    (hPut b " ")
    (hPut b name)
  }
)

; The `Vec` length, as the `for` templates name it. This module's own
; so the templates' free identifiers all resolve here (MAC-HYG-7), and
; PUBLIC because a template's free identifier is checked for
; visibility at the INVOCATION: measured, a private `hVecLen` made
; every `(for ...)` in an importing file `AX3023 private-name
; Html::hVecLen`. See the header's third point.
(pub :: hVecLen (-> (Vec a) Int))

(pub fn (hVecLen v) (vecLen v))

; Element `i` of a `Vec` of Strings, as `for` reads it.
(pub :: hVecStr (-> (Vec a) Int String))

(pub fn (hVecStr v i) (vecGetStr v i))

; Element `i` of a `Vec` of Ints, as `forInt` reads it.
(pub :: hVecWord (-> (Vec Int) Int Int))

(pub fn (hVecWord v i) (vecGet v i))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The element forms. Four macros define the whole vocabulary; the tag
; table below is one line per spelling forwarding to one of them.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; `(el b "tag" { children })` - an element with children and no
; attributes. The builder and the tag are bound first (MAC-SAFE-1):
; each is mentioned twice in the template.
(pub macro (el b tag body) (let (
  (bb b)
  (tt tag)
)
  {
    (hOpenSolo bb tt)
    body
    (hClose bb tt)
  }
))

; `(elA b "tag" { attributes } { children })` - an element with an
; attribute block, written between `<tag` and `>`, then its children.
(pub macro (elA b tag attrs body) (let (
  (bb b)
  (tt tag)
)
  {
    (hOpen bb tt)
    attrs
    (hOpenEnd bb)
    body
    (hClose bb tt)
  }
))

; `(elVoid b "tag")` - a void element, `<br>`, with nothing inside
; and no closing tag.
(pub macro (elVoid b tag) (hOpenSolo b tag))

; `(elVoidA b "tag" { attributes })` - a void element with attributes,
; `<img src="..." alt="...">`.
(pub macro (elVoidA b tag attrs) (let ((bb b))
  {
    (hOpen bb tag)
    attrs
    (hOpenEnd bb)
  }
))

; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; The tag table: `(tag b { children })` and `(tagA b { attributes }
; { children })` for each. Written out by hand, for the header's fifth
; point. A tag that is not here is `(el b "tag" ...)`.
; ------------------------------------------------------------------
; `<html>`
(pub macro (html b k) (el b "html" k))

; `<html ...>`
(pub macro (htmlA b attrs k) (elA b "html" attrs k))

; `<head>`
(pub macro (head b k) (el b "head" k))

; `<head ...>`
(pub macro (headA b attrs k) (elA b "head" attrs k))

; `<title>`
(pub macro (title b k) (el b "title" k))

; `<title ...>`
(pub macro (titleA b attrs k) (elA b "title" attrs k))

; `<body>`
(pub macro (body b k) (el b "body" k))

; `<body ...>`
(pub macro (bodyA b attrs k) (elA b "body" attrs k))

; `<div>`
(pub macro (div b k) (el b "div" k))

; `<div ...>`
(pub macro (divA b attrs k) (elA b "div" attrs k))

; `<p>`
(pub macro (p b k) (el b "p" k))

; `<p ...>`
(pub macro (pA b attrs k) (elA b "p" attrs k))

; `<span>`
(pub macro (span b k) (el b "span" k))

; `<span ...>`
(pub macro (spanA b attrs k) (elA b "span" attrs k))

; `<a>` - spelled `anchor`, because `a` is a type variable in the
; standard library's own signatures (the header's list).
(pub macro (anchor b k) (el b "a" k))

; `<a href="...">`
(pub macro (anchorA b attrs k) (elA b "a" attrs k))

; `<ul>`
(pub macro (ul b k) (el b "ul" k))

; `<ul ...>`
(pub macro (ulA b attrs k) (elA b "ul" attrs k))

; `<ol>`
(pub macro (ol b k) (el b "ol" k))

; `<ol ...>`
(pub macro (olA b attrs k) (elA b "ol" attrs k))

; `<li>`
(pub macro (li b k) (el b "li" k))

; `<li ...>`
(pub macro (liA b attrs k) (elA b "li" attrs k))

; `<h1>`
(pub macro (h1 b k) (el b "h1" k))

; `<h1 ...>`
(pub macro (h1A b attrs k) (elA b "h1" attrs k))

; `<h2>`
